\section{Relationships Between the Classes}
The main relationships in the project can be summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item \textbf{\textcolor{accent}{Inheritance:}} \code{Hero}, \code{Monster} and \code{Bullet} inherit from \code{pygame.sprite.Sprite}.
  \item \textbf{\textcolor{accent}{Association (has-a):}} a \code{Hero} object keeps references to the \code{Settings} object and to the display surface \code{screen}.
  \item \textbf{\textcolor{accent}{Association:}} a \code{HUD} object keeps references to \code{Settings}, \code{screen}, \code{GameStats} and \code{Hero}.
  \item \textbf{\textcolor{accent}{Association:}} a \code{GameStats} object keeps a reference to \code{Settings}.
  \item \textbf{\textcolor{accent}{Creation and coordination:}} \code{main.py} creates the objects, connects them, and calls their methods together with the functions in \code{game\_logic.py} (imported as \code{gl}).
  \item \textbf{\textcolor{accent}{Group:}} the \code{bullets} group manages all \code{Bullet} objects. This works because \code{Bullet} inherits from \code{Sprite}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Creating the objects in main.py}
\begin{lstlisting}
settings = Settings()
play_button = Button(settings, screen, "Play")
stats = GameStats(settings)
hero = Hero(settings, screen)
monster = Monster(settings, screen)
hud = HUD(settings, screen, stats, hero)
bullets = Group()
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item \code{Settings()} creates the settings object and stores it in \code{settings}.
  \item \code{Hero(settings, screen)} creates the hero and passes it the settings and the screen.
  \item \code{Monster(settings, screen)} creates the monster.
  \item \code{GameStats(settings)} creates an object that tracks the game state and the score.
  \item \code{HUD(settings, screen, stats, hero)} creates the information display and passes it the data it needs.
  \item \code{Group()} creates a group that manages the bullets.
\end{itemize}

% =====================================================================
\section{Concept 1: Inheritance}
Inheritance means that a subclass receives attributes and behaviors from a parent class. In this project \code{Sprite} (provided by Pygame) is the parent class, and \code{Hero}, \code{Monster} and \code{Bullet} are its subclasses.

\begin{lstlisting}
from pygame.sprite import Sprite

class Hero(Sprite):
    """The player's character."""

    def __init__(self, settings, screen):
        super().__init__()
        self.screen = screen
        self.settings = settings

        self.image = pygame.image.load('images/mega-ball.png')
        self.rect = self.image.get_rect()
        self.screen_rect = screen.get_rect()

        self.reset()
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item \code{class Hero(Sprite)} declares \code{Hero} as a subclass of \code{Sprite}.
  \item \code{super().\_\_init\_\_()} calls the parent initializer so the inherited part of the object is set up correctly.
  \item \code{self.screen} and \code{self.settings} store references to the screen and the settings inside the hero object.
  \item \code{self.rect} gives the hero a position and size used for movement and collisions.
\end{itemize}

The same pattern appears in \code{Monster} and \code{Bullet}:

\begin{lstlisting}
class Bullet(Sprite):
    """A bullet fired by the hero."""

    def __init__(self, settings, screen, hero):
        super().__init__()
        self.screen = screen

        self.rect = pygame.Rect(0, 0, settings.bullet_width,
                                     settings.bullet_height)
        self.color = settings.bullet_color
        self.rect.centerx = hero.rect.centerx
        self.rect.bottom = hero.rect.top
\end{lstlisting}

\textbf{\textcolor{accent}{Why is inheritance useful here?}} Because the hero, the monster and the bullets are all of type \code{Sprite}, the bullets can be stored in a \code{pygame.sprite.Group}, and functions such as \code{pygame.sprite.collide\_rect} and \code{spritecollideany} can work with them. Inheritance does not draw or move anything automatically; those behaviors are written by the project in its own methods.

% =====================================================================
\section{Concept 2: Encapsulation}
Encapsulation means keeping data and the behavior that works on it together inside a class, and providing methods to use it, instead of scattering the logic everywhere. Python has no strict private attributes; instead, a name that starts with a single underscore (such as \code{\_chase}) is used by convention to mean ``internal''.

\begin{lstlisting}
class Hero(Sprite):
    def reset(self):
        """Put the hero at the bottom center with full health and ammo."""
        self.rect.centerx = self.screen_rect.centerx
        self.rect.bottom = self.screen_rect.bottom
        self.center_x = float(self.rect.centerx)
        self.center_y = float(self.rect.centery)

        self.moving_right = False
        self.moving_left = False
        self.moving_up = False
        self.moving_down = False

        self.health = self.settings.hero_health
        self.ammo = self.settings.ammo_limit
        self.last_hit_time = -10 ** 9

    def take_damage(self):
        """Lose one health point unless still protected."""
        now = pygame.time.get_ticks()
        if now - self.last_hit_time > self.settings.hit_cooldown:
            self.health -= 1
            self.last_hit_time = now
            return True
        return False
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item \code{health}, \code{ammo} and \code{last\_hit\_time} are data that belong to the hero's state.
  \item \code{reset()} gathers all the initialization of position, movement flags, health and ammo in one place.
  \item \code{take\_damage()} contains the damage rules, including the protection period \code{hit\_cooldown}.
  \item It returns \code{True} if a health point was removed and \code{False} if the hero is still protected.
\end{itemize}

Another example is the monster's chase behavior:

\begin{lstlisting}
def _chase(self, hero):
    dx = hero.rect.centerx - self.rect.centerx
    dy = hero.rect.centery - self.rect.centery
    dist = math.hypot(dx, dy)
    if dist > 0:
        speed = self.settings.monster_chase_speed
        self.x += dx / dist * speed
        self.y += dy / dist * speed
\end{lstlisting}

The name \code{\_chase} signals that the method is an internal detail of \code{Monster}; together with \code{\_patrol} and \code{\_decide\_dodge} it is called only from \code{Monster.update()}. The underscore does not technically prevent outside calls; it is a signal to other programmers.

% =====================================================================
\section{Concept 3: Abstraction}
Abstraction means exposing the operation that other parts of the program need to call while hiding the details of how it is done. \code{main.py} does not need to know the math of moving the hero; it only calls \code{hero.update()}.

\begin{lstlisting}
if stats.state == "playing":
    hero.update()
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item The program checks that the game state is \code{"playing"}.
  \item It calls \code{hero.update()} to update the hero.
  \item The details (speed, screen limits, updating coordinates) live inside the \code{Hero} class, not in \code{main.py}.
\end{itemize}

\begin{lstlisting}
def update(self, bullets, hero):
    now = pygame.time.get_ticks()

    # Hero has no way to hurt it anymore: hunt him down.
    if hero.ammo == 0 and len(bullets) == 0:
        self._chase(hero)
    else:
        self._patrol(bullets, now)

    self.rect.x = self.x
    self.rect.y = self.y
\end{lstlisting}

Here \code{Monster.update()} hides the monster's decision making (chase, patrol, dodge) behind a single call. The same idea appears in the game loop:

\begin{lstlisting}
gl.update_monster(settings, stats, hero, monster, bullets)
\end{lstlisting}

\code{main.py} calls a clearly named function from \code{game\_logic} without writing the monster-update and collision logic in the main loop. Here abstraction is built on functions and methods rather than on an Abstract Base Class.

% =====================================================================
\section{Concept 4: Polymorphism}
Polymorphism means that one general call can produce different behavior depending on the type of the object. Typically several classes inherit from one parent and override the same method with different implementations.

The project has one real example. In \code{update\_bullets()} the call is made on the whole group:

\begin{lstlisting}
def update_bullets(settings, stats, hud, hero, monster, bullets):
    bullets.update()
    ...
\end{lstlisting}

\code{Group.update()} calls the \code{update()} method of every sprite it contains. Each class defines \code{update()} in its own way:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item \code{Hero.update()} moves the hero according to the pressed keys.
  \item \code{Bullet.update()} moves the bullet up the screen.
  \item \code{Monster.update(bullets, hero)} patrols, dodges or chases.
\end{itemize}

However, \code{Monster.update()} takes extra parameters, so the project does not call \code{update()} uniformly on all three classes. A clearer polymorphic design would give them a common drawing method. In the current code the hero and monster use \code{blitme()} and the bullet uses \code{draw\_bullet()}; the following example (not in the project) shows the idea:

\begin{lstlisting}
class Hero(Sprite):
    def draw(self):
        self.screen.blit(self.image, self.rect)

class Monster(Sprite):
    def draw(self):
        self.screen.blit(self.image, self.rect)

class Bullet(Sprite):
    def draw(self):
        pygame.draw.ellipse(self.screen, self.color, self.rect)
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
  \item All three classes would offer a method named \code{draw()}.
  \item The drawing differs: the hero and monster draw an image, while the bullet draws an ellipse.
  \item The same call \code{obj.draw()} could then be used on any of them, and each type behaves in its own way.
\end{itemize}

